\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
Montgomery-squaring subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a fresh
successor state and adding an assumption characterising the squared result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} index/offset selecting which instance of the
subroutine block to step over; it affects the program-counter offset and which
temporaries are carried. The second is a {string list} naming the output
variable and the input variable of the squaring step; these names are used in
the new result assumption and in the memory-read assumption for the output
buffer. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is {eventually ... s} for a current
state {s}, the tactic produces the same conclusion with the successor state
{s'} in place of {s}:
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================================ LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC i [out; in]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained from {A} as follows: every state-dependent fact
about {s} — program counter, loaded-bytes fact, register and memory reads, and
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state — is discharged and
re-added in the corresponding form for {s'}, with the {MAYCHANGE} relation
extended by the components written by this step. A new memory-read assumption
records that the output buffer of {s'} holds the named result, and a new
implication assumption states that, under a bound hypothesis on the square of
the input, the named result equals the Montgomery product of the input with
itself modulo the prime. Purely arithmetic/nonoverlapping assumptions are kept,
sometimes reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this {eventually} shape with a full set of state assumptions.

\ENDDOC
